      {
        "id": 211022,
        "name": "dprcooke",
        "username": "dprcooke",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/dprcooke/{size}/1961_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-12-17T04:13:30.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Arcanist Lupus\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n Arcanist Lupus:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the benefits to having the heroes go before the villain are less then the downsides that come with letting the villains take two turns in a row.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>This.</p>\n<p>In some of the scenes, there could be quite a lot of villain minions, lieutenants, etc. The heroes all going first before the villains act won’t put a huge dent in the villains, but the villains going twice in a row will put a dent in the heroes.</p>\n<p>Besides, you’re creating a story together, and all the heroes going, then all the villians, doesn’t make for a very exciting fight scene/story, does it? (And if that’s not enough to convince your players, just kindly remind them that a villain double-turn will hurt, and that you won’t hesitate to make it hurt a LOT of that’s the kind of game they want to play.)</p>",
        "post_number": 4,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:41:28.613Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
