        "raw": "<p>It&#39;s sort of like this:</p><p><em>Start of round</em></p><p><em>Heroes all take their turns, passing to villains</em></p><p><em>Villains take their turns, passing to Environment</em></p><p><em>Environment take&#39;s its turn</em></p><p><em>End of round/Start of round</em></p><p><em>Environment passes to Villains, since Villains haven&#39;t gone this round</em></p><p><em>Villains take their turns, passing to the Environment</em></p><p><em>Environment take&#39;s its turn, passing(FINALLY) to the Heroes</em></p><p>If a DM feels like being a jerk, they can lump up all the opposing turns and really whail on the PCs/Heroes. That&#39;s why the book suggests to mix up the turns, trying to create a more interesting story than the standard &#39;everyone punches eachother&#39; that most RPGs boil down to.</p>",
