      {
        "id": 211024,
        "name": "speedyolrac",
        "username": "speedyolrac",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/speedyolrac/{size}/1918_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-12-19T13:00:54.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I have done that in round 5 to let the villains steal a thing and leave the scene. Left enough underling to slow down the heroes so the villains and get away with the plans for Project SteelChase. A few days later they had to <span>find the&nbsp;</span>hideout and fight the robots made form the stolen plans. It was a difficult battle, but they manage to recover from twice. (They are yet to find out that the villain copied the plans and sold it, a plot brewing in the distance).</p><p>Once the let the environment&nbsp;go last near the end. I rolled a positive&nbsp;thing for the environment, the villain&nbsp;benefited&nbsp;from it and let the environment&nbsp;go next, changing&nbsp;it to something that benefited&nbsp;the villain. Then I let the Heroes go.</p><p>I would do it all the time but a well-timed double villain turn can make things interesting. Christopher said that he had a group that prefered&nbsp;going hero-environment-villain-environment-hero-hero repeat. not only do the villain go twice so do the heroes.</p>",
        "post_number": 6,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:41:28.760Z",
